<v Announcer>This American Life播客由Anheuser-Busch提供支持，隆重推出百威美式艾尔啤酒（Budweiser American Ale），选用来自太平洋西北地区的焦糖麦芽大麦和卡斯卡特啤酒花。详情请访问budamericanale.com。此外还有audible.com，您可以在此下载最畅销的有声读物、杂志和广播节目（如本节目）到您的iPod或MP3播放器。立即访问audible.com/thisamericanlife，获取我们过去10年及更多的精彩存档。

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:34.270
<v Ira Glass>When holiday season began last November, Yvonne started getting it from all sides, friends, family.
<v Ira Glass>去年11月假日季开始时，Yvonne开始从四面八方感受到来自朋友和家人的压力。

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:59.170
<v Yvonne>I think people look at me sometimes, because I live alone, and think that I'm lonely. Like, tomorrow is Thanksgiving, and I had to really convince everybody that it's OK. I'm going to be home alone on Thanksgiving. Go to Virginia, go to auntie whomever. Do your thing. And I'm going to be fine. Well, you know. No, I'm really going to be fine.
<v Yvonne>我觉得人们有时看着我，因为我一个人住，就觉得我很孤独。比如，明天是感恩节，我不得不费尽心思去说服每个人这没关系。感恩节那天我会一个人在家。去弗吉尼亚吧，去探望哪位阿姨吧。去做你们自己的事。我会过得很好。好吧，你知道的。不，我真的会过得很好。

00:00:59.170 --> 00:01:09.150
<v Ira Glass>Yvonne talked to researcher Allison McKim and our producer, John Jeter. She's lived alone since her kids moved out 12 years ago, and she says it took her five years before she came to actually enjoy it.
<v Ira Glass>Yvonne接受了研究员Allison McKim和我们的制片人John Jeter的采访。自12年前孩子搬走后，她一直独居，她说她花了五年时间才真正开始享受这种生活。

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:33.760
<v Yvonne>I like the fact that it is self-indulgent. What I want to eat, whether I choose to talk to anybody on the phone, if I want the TV and the radio going at the same time. And I'm not looking at or listening to either one.
<v Yvonne>我喜欢这种随心所欲的感觉。想吃什么就吃什么，想不想跟人通电话，想不想电视和收音机同时开着，哪怕我根本没在看也没在听。

00:01:33.760 --> 00:02:01.420
<v Yvonne>I enjoy cooking, so cooking is not an issue, per se. But I don't want to have to do anything. And so, I would want to cook when I want to, and I don't even want to compromise on it. OK, so you do Wednesday and Thursday, and I'll do-- No, I'll do it when I feel like it. I want to do everything I feel like doing when I feel like doing it.
<v Yvonne>我喜欢做饭，所以做饭本身不是问题。但我不想不得不去做任何事。所以，我想做饭的时候就做，我甚至不想在这上面妥协。好吧，你做周三和周四，我做——不，我随心所欲地做。我只想在我有心情的时候做我想做的一切。

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:16.150
<v Yvonne>It's like killing a waterbug. I'm going to run from it if I'm here by myself. But if, like you two are here, I was like, oh, no. I'll get it. I'll get it. Where's the spray? I can do that with other people, but I can just be the coward that I am when I'm alone.
<v Yvonne>就像打死一只水虫。如果我自己一个人在这儿，我会躲得远远的。但如果你们两个在场，我会说，噢，没事。我来处理。我来处理。喷雾在哪儿？有别人在时我能做到，但只有我一个人时，我就可以做个彻头彻尾的胆小鬼。

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:18.040
<v Allison Mckim>And you don't have to save face.
<v Allison Mckim>而且你不需要顾及面子。

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:21.150
<v Yvonne>Right, exactly. Exactly.
<v Yvonne>对，没错。确实如此。

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:42.590
<v Ira Glass>When she gets involved with men, they're perfectly fine, she says. But eventually she always decides that she would enjoy being alone more. Though, at first, she was scared to live alone, scared that somebody was going to break in, scared about how she was going to fill her time. That took some getting used to. And these days, she's afraid what's going to happen to her when she gets older, which is something her daughter worries about, too.
<v Ira Glass>她说，当她与男性交往时，他们表现得很好。但最终她总是决定自己一个人过更快乐。虽然起初她很害怕独居，怕有人闯入，怕不知道该怎么打发时间。那需要时间适应。如今，她担心自己变老后会发生什么，这也是她女儿所担心的。

00:02:42.590 --> 00:03:16.730
<v Yvonne>Yeah, she's worried that I'm going to be old, broke, possibly sick, and maybe unhappy that I've made this choice. Like, the rest of them, they want to see me happy, and they don't believe that I can be happy unless I have a companion.
<v Yvonne>是啊，她担心我会变老、破产、可能生病，而且可能对做出了这个选择感到不开心。就像其他人一样，他们想看到我幸福，他们不相信除非有个伴，否则我不可能幸福。

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:49.230
<v Yvonne>Every now and then, the thought occurs to me, what if they're right? It does happen every now and then. What if they're right? If this is as good as it gets, right now, and then I'm on to steady decline. You'll have to interview me in 20 years, when I'll say, why didn't you stop me then, when I had a chance? I don't know. I'm good right now.
<v Yvonne>时不时地，我会闪过一个念头：万一他们是对的呢？这种情况偶尔确实会发生。万一他们是对的呢？如果这就是目前最好的状况，而接下来我将稳步走下坡路。20年后你们得再来采访我，到时候我会说，那时候我还有机会，你们为什么不阻止我？我不知道。我现在过得很好。

00:03:49.230 --> 00:04:19.220
<v Ira Glass>Today in our radio show, Home Alone. We have three stories today of people living alone. Act One is the story of detective work in the homes of those who live by themselves. Act Two is about a teenager who ends up living all alone. Act three is about a woman who has to trick an armed man into doing the right thing. It's This American Life , from WBEZ Chicago, distributed by Public Radio International. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>今天我们的广播节目主题是《独自在家》。今天我们有三个关于独居人士的故事。第一幕是关于独居者家中侦探工作的故事。第二幕是关于一个最终独自生活的青少年的故事。第三幕是关于一个女人不得不诱骗一名武装男子做正确之事的经历。这是来自芝加哥WBEZ的《This American Life》，由公共广播国际（Public Radio International）发行。请继续收听。

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:47.540
<v Ira Glass>Act One, A Plot Without a Story. You may remember a couple years back in Chicago, in 1995, over 700 people died after a massive heat wave. And hundreds of them died alone, at home. Well, sociologist Eric Klinenberg wrote a book about all this, called Heat Wave . And he says people die alone like this all the time, all over the country. In fact, big cities have special personnel to deal with those deaths, who travel, Eric says, in the secret society of those who live and die alone.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，一个没有故事的情节。你可能还记得几年前的1995年，芝加哥在一次大规模热浪之后有700多人死亡。他们中数百人是在家中孤独离世的。社会学家Eric Klinenberg写了一本关于此事的书，名为《热浪》。他说全国各地的人们总是这样孤独离世。事实上，大城市有专门的人员处理这些死亡事件，他们穿梭在Eric所说的那些独居独死者的秘密社会中。

00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:57.400
<v Ira Glass>In Los Angeles, the county has an entire department, 100 people, just to manage the bodies and belongings of the deceased. And we sent Eric to LA, to watch what they do.
<v Ira Glass>在洛杉矶，县里有一个完整的部门，有100人，专门负责管理死者的遗体和遗物。我们派Eric去洛杉矶，看看他们是如何工作的。

00:04:57.400 --> 00:05:19.040
<v Eric Klinenberg>Mary Ann lived alone, and she died that way, too. She was 79 years old when she called herself an ambulance and went into the hospital. Her life ended there two weeks later, after a full cardiac arrest. She didn't have a friend or relative at her side. In fact, the only person she'd even listed as an emergency contact was this woman, Sue, who delivered drugs for her pharmacy.
<v Eric Klinenberg>Mary Ann独居，最终也孤独离世。她79岁时自己叫了救护车去了医院。两周后，在经历了一次全面心搏停止后，她的生命在那里结束了。她身边没有朋友或亲戚。事实上，她唯一列为紧急联系人的人是这位女士Sue，她是药房的送药员。

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:31.520
<v Sue>I was really surprised when they called me and said that my name was on there as the person to contact. I went, oh, really. OK. I didn't even know if she had a brother or had family.
<v Sue>当他们给我打电话说我的名字出现在上面作为联系人时，我真的很惊讶。我说，噢，真的吗？好吧。我甚至不知道她是否有兄弟姐妹或家人。

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:40.550
<v Eric Klinenberg>Sue last heard from Mary Ann on a phone call from the hospital. She left an urgent message, pleading with Sue to feed and look after her dogs, who were chained up outside the house.
<v Eric Klinenberg>Sue最后一次收到Mary Ann的消息是从医院打来的电话。她留下一条紧急信息，恳求Sue去喂养和照看她那些被拴在屋外的狗。

00:05:40.550 --> 00:06:17.210
<v Sue>When she called, she was just crying, because she said, if somebody doesn't pick up the mail, they'll take her dogs away. And she said, they're all I have. She goes, I promise I won't cheat you. I'll pay you. I wasn't worried about that. It just broke my heart a little bit, you know, she would think that that's all she had. That's what she said.
<v Sue>她打电话时一直在哭，因为她说，如果没人去取邮件，他们就会把她的狗带走。她说，它们是我仅有的全部了。她说，我保证我不会亏待你。我会付钱给你。我担心的不是那个。这让我有点心碎，你知道，她竟然认为那是她所有的东西。这就是她说的。

00:06:17.210 --> 00:06:25.680
<v Eric Klinenberg>This is the hospital where Mary Ann died. It's been two weeks, and her body's still here. No one's planned a funeral, or even picked up her things.
<v Eric Klinenberg>这就是Mary Ann去世的医院。已经两周了，她的遗体还在这里。没有人计划葬礼，甚至没有人来取走她的物品。

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:32.410
<v Emily>The only personal property left at the hospital, according to their records, is a purse with glasses.
<v Emily>根据记录，医院里留下的唯一个人财产是一个装着眼镜的钱包。

00:06:32.410 --> 00:06:51.180
<v Eric Klinenberg>This is Emily [? Issa ?]. She's a deputy investigator for the Los Angeles County Public Administrator, which means she's sort of like a detective for people who die alone. Emily combs through the remains of people's lives, trying to figure out what they left behind and who should get it, searching for next of kin. Emily and her colleagues get 3,000 cases each year.
<v Eric Klinenberg>这是Emily [? Issa ?]。她是洛杉矶县公共行政官的一名副调查员，这意味着她有点像专门处理独居离世者案件的侦探。Emily仔细搜寻逝者生活的遗迹，试图弄清楚他们留下了什么，谁应该得到这些，并寻找近亲。Emily和她的同事每年处理3000起案件。

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:56.870
<v Emily>Hi. I'm here to see Carla Moreno in Patient Services, to pick up some property for a decedent.
<v Emily>您好。我来找患者服务部的Carla Moreno，为一位逝者领取一些财产。

00:06:56.870 --> 00:07:21.290
<v Eric Klinenberg>In this case, Mary Ann was single, with no known siblings or children. But she owned her house. She had a bank account, and who knows what other valuables in her home. Now someone stands to inherit it, and it's Emily's job to find out who. She's looking for someone who knew Mary Ann, to lead her to a relative. There's also the question of who's going to bury her. Before Emily's finished, she wants to resolve that, too.
<v Eric Klinenberg>在这个案例中，Mary Ann单身，没有已知的兄弟姐妹或子女。但她有自己的房子。她有一个银行账户，谁知道家里还有什么其他贵重物品。现在有人有权继承这些，Emily的工作就是查出那是谁。她正在寻找认识Mary Ann的人，以引导她找到亲属。还有一个问题是，谁来埋葬她。在Emily工作完成前，她也想解决这个问题。

00:07:21.290 --> 00:07:31.090
<v Eric Klinenberg>We start our search at the Patient Services Office. There's a nun working there. And, at first, we think she might know something helpful, because when she sees Mary Ann's name on the case file, her face lights up.
<v Eric Klinenberg>我们从患者服务办公室开始搜索。那里有一位修女在工作。起初，我们认为她可能知道一些有用的信息，因为当她在案件档案上看到Mary Ann的名字时，她的脸亮了起来。

00:07:31.090 --> 00:07:34.000
<v Nun>Oh. I know her.
<v Nun>噢。我认识她。

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:36.340
<v Emily>How well did you know her?
<v Emily>您有多了解她？

00:07:36.340 --> 00:07:43.910
<v Nun>I just met her a couple of times, was able to visit her. Yeah. Just when she was here. Anyway, so let's just have you--
<v Nun>我只是见过她几次，能够探望她。是的。就在她在这里的时候。总之，让我们……

00:07:43.910 --> 00:07:46.490
<v Emily>She ever mention anything about family to you? Did she ever--
<v Emily>她有没有向您提过任何关于家人的事？她有没有……

00:07:46.490 --> 00:07:48.900
<v Nun>No.
<v Nun>没有。

00:07:48.900 --> 00:08:00.070
<v Eric Klinenberg>The nun hands Emily a big plastic bag. Inside is everything Mary Ann brought with her to the hospital, and Emily starts combing through it, looking for clues. There's a fluffy blue robe, a small black purse.
<v Eric Klinenberg>修女递给Emily一个大塑料袋。里面是Mary Ann带到医院的所有东西，Emily开始仔细搜寻，寻找线索。有一件毛茸茸的蓝色长袍，一个小黑钱包。

00:08:00.070 --> 00:08:17.360
<v Emily>There's some medication, some baby powder, just some glasses, typical things you'd find in a female's purse. These are coupons. Here's the ads that she got the coupons from.
<v Emily>有一些药，一些婴儿爽身粉，只是一些眼镜，你在女士钱包里能找到的典型东西。这些是优惠券。这些是她获取优惠券的广告单。

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:47.620
<v Eric Klinenberg>None of this is of much use to Emily. She needs contact information, an address book, a cell phone with some names on the speed-dial. There's nothing like that in the purse. She finds a notebook, and she flips through it in search of a personal note, maybe a list of last wishes. But every page is blank. What she does get is a set of keys to Mary Ann's house. And Emily tells me that the best-case scenario is that, once we get there, we'll find a will or some instructions. That only happens rarely, and in her line of work, it's like hitting the jackpot.
<v Eric Klinenberg>这些对Emily来说都没什么用。她需要联系方式，通讯录，手机里的快速拨号名单。钱包里什么都没有。她找到一个笔记本，翻看着寻找个人笔记，也许是一份遗愿清单。但每一页都是空白的。她确实得到了一串Mary Ann房子的钥匙。Emily告诉我，最好的情况是，一旦我们到达那里，能找到遗嘱或一些指示。这很少发生，在她的工作中，这就像中了大奖。

00:08:47.620 --> 00:09:00.030
<v Emily>I've been out to a case where I walked in, and on the night stand next to the bed, it said "In case of emergency." And I opened the envelope, and it had listed everything that I would want to know. And I'm like, wow. That's five minutes and I'm done.
<v Emily>我曾去过一个案发现场，走进屋子，床边的床头柜上写着“紧急情况下”。我打开信封，里面列出了我想知道的一切。我说，哇。五分钟我就搞定了。

00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:28.350
<v Eric Klinenberg>Mary Ann's case isn't that easy. When we get to her house, the two dogs are still chained up in the yard, so Emily calls animal control to take them away. The outside of Mary Ann's house is a mess. The wood panels are rotting. There's powdery gray dirt where grass should be. An old Volkswagen minibus with flat tires sits in the dusty driveway.
<v Eric Klinenberg>Mary Ann的案子没那么容易。当我们到达她家时，那两只狗仍然被拴在院子里，所以Emily打电话给动物管理局把它们带走。Mary Ann房子的外部一团糟。木板正在腐烂。本该是草地的地方是一片灰色的尘土。一辆轮胎瘪了的老式大众小巴停在尘土飞扬的车道上。

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:38.340
<v Eric Klinenberg>The inside's even worse. It's dark and dusty, cluttered with stacks of video cassettes, empty juice cans, boxes from the Home Shopping Network, many never even opened.
<v Eric Klinenberg>内部更糟。昏暗且满是灰尘，堆满了录像带、空果汁罐、电视购物频道的盒子，很多甚至从来没打开过。

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:42.600
<v Emily>I'm putting on my gloves so that we can start looking through the house.
<v Emily>我正在戴上手套，这样我们就可以开始搜查房子了。

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:48.110
<v Eric Klinenberg>Emily, who seems completely unfazed by all this, declares it mildly pack-rat.
<v Eric Klinenberg>Emily对这一切似乎完全没受影响，称其为轻微的囤积。

00:09:48.110 --> 00:09:52.350
<v Eric Klinenberg>This seems really pack-rat to me. There's not an inch of--
<v Eric Klinenberg>这在我看来确实是囤积。没有一寸地方……

00:09:52.350 --> 00:10:02.240
<v Emily>You can walk on the ground. You can still see the ground. We see plenty of cases where you can't even walk on the ground. There is no floor, because it's packed with stuff where you're climbing over.
<v Emily>你可以走在地上。你还能看到地面。我们见过很多案例，你甚至没法踩在地上。没有地板，因为到处堆满了东西，你得爬过去。

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:15.750
<v Eric Klinenberg>Emily is so used to places like this that she never goes on a search without gloves and tennis shoes, not to mention a mask, in case the person died at home and wasn't found for awhile. Usually, she has to dig around.
<v Eric Klinenberg>Emily对这样的地方习以为常，搜查时从不戴手套和穿网球鞋是不可能的，更别提戴上口罩了，以防有人死在家中且有一段时间没被发现。通常，她得四处挖掘。

00:10:15.750 --> 00:10:24.610
<v Eric Klinenberg>Emily doesn't just open drawers and medicine cabinets. If she has to, she climbs to the attic. She breaks down locked doors. She once found someone's business records in a refrigerator.
<v Eric Klinenberg>Emily不只是打开抽屉和药柜。如果必须的话，她会爬到阁楼。她会撞开锁着的门。她曾经在冰箱里找到过某人的商业记录。

00:10:24.610 --> 00:10:27.390
<v Emily>OK, so let me start poking around here.
<v Emily>好吧，让我开始在这里翻找一下。

00:10:27.390 --> 00:10:36.110
<v Eric Klinenberg>Emily is searching through Mary Ann's living room. You can tell she has basically condensed her life into this one area. There's an unmade makeshift day bed in front of the dusty television.
<v Eric Klinenberg>Emily正在搜查Mary Ann的客厅。你可以看出她基本上把生活浓缩在了这一块区域。杂乱的电视机前有一张没整理的临时日间床。

00:10:36.110 --> 00:10:50.030
<v Emily>So right now I'm just lifting up all of the different layers of the bed, the padding and blankets that are here. You'd be surprised how many times we actually find people that hide money and things under their bed.
<v Emily>所以现在我正在掀起床上的所有不同层，这里的衬垫和毯子。你会惊讶于我们实际上有多少次发现人们把钱和东西藏在床下。

00:10:50.030 --> 00:10:54.140
<v Eric Klinenberg>But there's nothing under there, just stacks of egg crates and musty blankets.
<v Eric Klinenberg>但下面什么都没有，只有成堆的蛋箱和发霉的毯子。

00:10:54.140 --> 00:10:56.470
<v Emily>Oh, back here. This is a table and chairs.
<v Emily>噢，在这后面。这是一张桌子和椅子。

00:10:56.470 --> 00:11:23.070
<v Eric Klinenberg>There's an entire dining room set hidden under the clutter. Emily seizes on a stack of mail and canceled checks and starts rifling through it, but she doesn't find what she's looking for. No business card from an attorney, no photos of friends or relatives, not even a personal check, just payments to AARP, Ladies Home Journal, TV Guide. In fact, there's not a single sign that there was another person in Mary Ann's life, and I find that much stranger than the mess.
<v Eric Klinenberg>杂物下隐藏着一整套餐厅家具。Emily抓住一叠邮件和已兑现的支票，开始翻阅，但她没有找到她要找的东西。没有律师的名片，没有朋友或亲戚的照片，甚至没有个人支票，只有给AARP、女士家庭杂志、电视指南的付款。事实上，没有半点迹象表明Mary Ann的生活中有另一个人存在，我发现这一点比混乱本身更奇怪。

00:11:23.070 --> 00:11:29.950
<v Eric Klinenberg>Is this unusual? I mean, we've now been here for maybe 45 minutes and we've not found a single personal item.
<v Eric Klinenberg>这不寻常吗？我的意思是，我们在这里已经待了大约45分钟，还没找到一件个人物品。

00:11:29.950 --> 00:11:30.460
<v Emily>Not at all.
<v Emily>一点也不。

00:11:30.460 --> 00:11:31.870
<v Eric Klinenberg>It's not unusual?
<v Eric Klinenberg>不寻常吗？

00:11:31.870 --> 00:11:56.120
<v Emily>Not at all. It's hard to describe, but there is a common thread that runs through a lot of our cases, where it's just like this, people surrounding themselves with things, things rather than people. But she's surrounded herself. She almost built herself into a little cave here, behind all of her stuff. And you can tell that this is where she spent most of her time.
<v Emily>一点也不。很难描述，但我们的很多案件中都有一个共同点，就是像这样，人们用东西把自己包围起来，是东西而不是人。但她包围了自己。她几乎把自己建在了一间小洞穴里，藏在她的东西后面。你可以看出这就是她大部分时间待的地方。

00:11:56.120 --> 00:12:16.290
<v Eric Klinenberg>Emily's just looking for contact information, not to piece together someone's life story. But sometimes she gets those stories anyway. She tells me about one case that she can't stop thinking about. A woman's husband died in World War II. She survived another 60 years, but her personal correspondence was a record of how she'd tried to avoid moving on.
<v Eric Klinenberg>Emily只是在寻找联系信息，而不是要拼凑某人的生平故事。但有时她还是会得知那些故事。她告诉我一个她无法停止思考的案例。一位女士的丈夫在二战中去世了。她又活了60年，但她的私人通信记录了她是如何试图避免继续前行的。

00:12:16.290 --> 00:12:47.870
<v Emily>I found the letters from the military telling her that his plane went down and that they couldn't find him. I found the letter that said, we found his plane now and he's still missing. And then I found the final letter that said, we found your husband's body. And she stopped living. Never remarried. They never had children. I mean, she just was-- it was like a time warp. She just completely cut off the rest of the world.
<v Emily>我找到了军方的信件，告诉她他的飞机坠毁了，他们找不到他。我找到了那封信，说，我们现在找到了他的飞机，但他仍然失踪。然后我找到了最后一封信，说，我们找到了你丈夫的遗体。她就停止了生活。再也没有结婚。他们从未有过孩子。我的意思是，她只是——就像一个时间扭曲。她完全切断了与世界其他地方的联系。

00:12:47.870 --> 00:13:15.680
<v Eric Klinenberg>It's remarkable how little we find out about Mary Ann. We learn that her mother once lived with her at this address. There's lots of mail to her. Her father married four times. Her mom was the third marriage. She was into herbal medicine and natural remedies. She had microchipped the dogs. Emily tells me she doesn't want to make a personal connection with the people she's investigating. She doesn't usually try to figure them out, because there are just so many of them. It would be hard to take. But I have to ask.
<v Eric Klinenberg>关于Mary Ann我们了解到的信息之少令人瞩目。我们了解到她的母亲曾经和她住在这个地址。有很多寄给她的邮件。她的父亲结过四次婚。她的母亲是第三任。她喜欢草药和自然疗法。她给狗植入了微芯片。Emily告诉我，她不想和她调查的人产生个人联系。她通常不去尝试弄明白他们，因为人实在太多了。那会很难接受。但我必须问。

00:13:15.680 --> 00:13:21.270
<v Eric Klinenberg>Do you ever wonder who she thought would clean this up?
<v Eric Klinenberg>您有没有想过她以为谁会来清理这些？

00:13:21.270 --> 00:13:34.760
<v Emily>No. It's never even crossed my mind to think of. Most of the time, you see these people don't want anybody in here. People who knew them never knew that they had this part of their lives that existed.
<v Emily>没有。我从来没想过。大多数时候，你会发现这些人不想让任何人进来。认识他们的人从来不知道他们生活中还有这部分存在。

00:13:34.760 --> 00:13:40.370
<v Eric Klinenberg>She must have known that someday she would die, and someone would come in and find all these things.
<v Eric Klinenberg>她一定知道总有一天她会去世，然后会有人进来发现所有这些东西。

00:13:40.370 --> 00:13:54.510
<v Emily>Maybe it's just part of her knowing that she has no one to leave it to. Where, who cares about my mess? Because I don't have anyone to leave my stuff anyway, so I might as well leave a mess.
<v Emily>也许这只是她知道自己没有人可托付的一部分。哪里，谁在乎我的烂摊子？因为我反正没人可以留东西给他们，所以我不如留下一片混乱。

00:13:54.510 --> 00:14:16.770
<v Eric Klinenberg>After nearly two hours, the only thing we found that might help Emily find a relative is a 30-year-old Christmas card addressed to Mary Ann and her mother. It's from a family in Virginia, and they must be related, because in the card they ask for help with the family tree for the kids. Emily deposits it in a clear plastic bag she'll bring back to the office.
<v Eric Klinenberg>近两个小时后，我们找到唯一可能帮助Emily找到亲属的东西是一张30年前寄给Mary Ann和她母亲的圣诞贺卡。它来自弗吉尼亚州的一个家庭，他们一定是亲戚，因为卡片里他们请求帮忙为孩子们整理家谱。Emily把它放进了一个透明的塑料袋里，准备带回办公室。

00:14:16.770 --> 00:14:23.750
<v Eric Klinenberg>Emily seals the front door and walks outside. A few neighbors are looking at us curiously, and she asked them what they know about Mary Ann.
<v Eric Klinenberg>Emily锁上前门走出屋外。几个邻居好奇地看着我们，她询问他们对Mary Ann了解多少。

00:14:23.750 --> 00:14:26.190
<v Emily>Did you ever see her? Did she have family?
<v Emily>您见过她吗？她有家人吗？

00:14:26.190 --> 00:14:30.350
<v Luis>After more than 20 years, I had never seen no visitors.
<v Luis>20多年了，我从来没见过访客。

00:14:30.350 --> 00:14:35.260
<v Eric Klinenberg>This is Luis, who owns the house next door, and he says Mary Ann was never all that friendly.
<v Eric Klinenberg>这是住在隔壁的Luis，他说Mary Ann一直不太友好。

00:14:35.260 --> 00:14:49.390
<v Luis>She doesn't seems to be very happy lady. Lonely. A lonely person is not a happy person. She was very lonely most of the years.
<v Luis>她看起来不是个非常快乐的女人。孤独。孤独的人不是快乐的人。这些年来她一直很孤独。

00:14:49.390 --> 00:14:54.130
<v Eric Klinenberg>George, another neighbor, had a different take. He thought she was happy.
<v Eric Klinenberg>另一位邻居George有不同的看法。他认为她很幸福。

00:14:54.130 --> 00:15:11.190
<v George>Yeah, all the time she talked with us. She talked with my son. And every day I come from work, about 3:30, 4 o'clock, and she sometimes on the porch. Hi, Mary. Hi, George.
<v George>是的，她一直和我们交谈。她和我的儿子交谈。每天我下班回来，大约3:30、4点，她有时在门廊上。嗨，Mary。嗨，George。

00:15:11.190 --> 00:15:49.300
<v Eric Klinenberg>So George thinks Mary Ann was happy. Luis thinks unhappy. Emily tells me this probably says more about Luis and George, and their personal feelings about people who are alone than Mary Ann. There's a stigma against living alone, and an even stronger one against dying alone. I think one reason Emily is so well-suited for her job is that she believes most people live the way they want to live. She may be right, but I'm not so sure. When someone keeps to herself, there's no way to know whether she lived and died outside the reach of friends and family because she preferred it that way or because of things beyond her control.
<v Eric Klinenberg>所以George认为Mary Ann很快乐。Luis认为不快乐。Emily告诉我，这可能更多地反映了Luis和George，以及他们对独居者的个人感受，而不是Mary Ann本身。社会对独居有偏见，对孤独离世的偏见更大。我认为Emily适合这项工作的一个原因是，她相信大多数人过着他们想要的生活。她可能是对的，但我不太确定。当一个人保持孤立时，无法得知她是出于偏好还是因为无法控制的事物而生活和死在亲友触及不到的地方。

00:15:49.300 --> 00:15:54.470
<v Emily>Good afternoon. My name is Emily [? Issa ?]. I'm a deputy calling from the Los Angeles County--
<v Emily>下午好。我叫Emily [? Issa ?]。我是洛杉矶县的一名副调查员，致电……

00:15:54.470 --> 00:16:20.520
<v Eric Klinenberg>Back in her cubicle, Emily makes a bunch of phone calls. She tracks down the number of the man whose name was on the one clue she found in Mary Ann's house, the 30-year-old Christmas card. His name is Terry. He asked me not to use his last name. But when Emily gets him on the phone, there's a problem. He's got no idea who Mary Ann is. Terry tells Emily to call his ex-wife. She's the one who actually wrote the card. Moments later, Emily's talking to her.
<v Eric Klinenberg>回到工位上，Emily打了很多电话。她追踪到了Mary Ann家里发现的唯一线索——那张30年前的圣诞贺卡上那个人的号码。他的名字叫Terry。他请求我不要使用他的姓氏。但当Emily接通电话时，出了问题。他根本不知道Mary Ann是谁。Terry让Emily给他的前妻打电话。那张卡片其实是她写的。片刻之后，Emily正在和她通话。

00:16:20.520 --> 00:16:30.860
<v Emily>Oh, he's the one that gave me the phone number for you, and he said he had never heard of her. So you think that she was his aunt. I see.
<v Emily>噢，是他给了我您的电话号码，他说他从来没听说过她。所以您认为她是他的姑妈。我明白了。

00:16:30.860 --> 00:16:42.500
<v Eric Klinenberg>The ex-wife was partly right. Mary Ann was a relative Terry's, but a distant one. Her mother was Terry's great aunt. But Terry had never met Mary Ann, never even spoken to her. And he knew virtually nothing about her.
<v Eric Klinenberg>那位前妻有一部分是对的。Mary Ann是Terry的亲戚，但很疏远。她的母亲是Terry的姑婆。但Terry从未见过Mary Ann，甚至从未和她说过话。他对她几乎一无所知。

00:16:42.500 --> 00:16:57.950
<v Terry>We've been just going back and forth on this, trying to figure out, who is this person? And meantime, I went back to the genealogy records, and I found all these Christmas cards. And lo and behold, it was Mary Ann and her mother.
<v Terry>我们一直在来回确认，试图搞清楚，这人是谁？与此同时，我查了系谱记录，找到了所有这些圣诞贺卡。你猜怎么着，上面正是Mary Ann和她母亲。

00:16:57.950 --> 00:17:02.190
<v Eric Klinenberg>Terry doesn't hesitate to admit that it was hard to feel emotional about Mary Ann's death.
<v Eric Klinenberg>Terry毫不犹豫地承认，对Mary Ann的去世感到悲伤很难。

00:17:02.190 --> 00:17:14.680
<v Terry>My only feeling towards this right now is I feel responsibility to try to resolve her situation, to tidy up her life, I guess.
<v Terry>我现在对这件事唯一的感受是，我感到有责任去处理她的情况，我想，清理她的生活。

00:17:14.680 --> 00:17:37.380
<v Eric Klinenberg>One month after Mary Ann died, Terry and his cousins were still deciding whether they wanted the county to settle the estate or handle it themselves. Emily told me that, because Mary Ann's assets total more than $6,000, the Public Administrator would arrange for her to be buried in a local cemetery. But what if she didn't have $6,000? LA County would still take care of things.
<v Eric Klinenberg>Mary Ann去世一个月后，Terry和他的表亲们仍在决定他们是想让县政府处理遗产还是自己处理。Emily告诉我，因为Mary Ann的资产总额超过6000美元，公共行政官会安排她在当地墓地安葬。但如果她没有6000美元呢？洛杉矶县仍然会处理这些事务。

00:17:37.380 --> 00:17:55.830
<v Chaplain>Honored guests, on this day we are gathered here for the annual mass burial, committing to this earthly resting place 1,918 brothers and sisters of humankind who passed away--
<v Chaplain>尊贵的各位嘉宾，今天我们在此举行年度集体葬礼，将1918位离世的兄弟姐妹安葬在这片大地……

00:17:55.830 --> 00:18:15.000
<v Eric Klinenberg>When people die alone here without the money for burial, their bodies are cremated, and their ashes are stored in individual boxes for four years. After that, if no one claims them, the ashes are removed from the boxes and buried together in a mass grave. The burial takes place once a year. This year's ceremony happened just a couple weeks ago.
<v Eric Klinenberg>当人们在这里孤独离世且没有钱支付丧葬费时，他们的遗体会被火化，骨灰被装在独立的盒子里存放四年。四年后，如果没有人认领，骨灰就会被从盒子里取出，合葬在一个集体墓穴中。葬礼每年举行一次。今年的仪式就在几周前举行。

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:23.720
<v Chaplain>We desire to honor these lives and the deaths of our unclaimed and seemingly forgotten people. Just as--
<v Chaplain>我们希望能缅怀这些无主且似乎被遗忘的人们的生命与逝去。正如……

00:18:23.720 --> 00:18:46.220
<v Eric Klinenberg>We're in the corner of a massive cemetery, right next to a construction site that's torn up the whole street. The service is heartfelt, but a little empty, too. Besides the chaplain, only 10 people, all county employees, showed up for the service. One of them pointed out the tiny plaques that mark each year's grave site. The remains of 1,900 people fill a hole that's 10 feet long, eight feet wide, eight feet deep.
<v Eric Klinenberg>我们在巨大的公墓角落里，就在拆毁了整条街道的施工现场旁边。仪式很真诚，但也有些空旷。除了牧师，只有10个人，都是县里的雇员，出席了仪式。其中一人指出了标记着每年墓地的细小铭牌。1900人的遗骸填满了一个10英尺长、8英尺宽、8英尺深的洞。

00:18:46.220 --> 00:19:14.830
<v Man>Look right over here. Half covered in dirt and sand, you've got 1984, and then three feet away is 1969. And there's 1966. And if we walk up this road, the numbers are going to keep dropping off and off and off, and go decades back. And you think about right here, in the span of just three feet, are the remains of thousands of people. And from 10 feet away, you would never know that these plaques are here.
<v Man>看这儿。覆盖在泥土和沙子里一半，这是1984年，然后在三英尺外是1969年。那是1966年。如果我们沿着这条路走，数字会不断减少，回溯到几十年前。你会想到就在这儿，仅仅三英尺的跨度内，是数千人的遗骸。离十英尺远，你永远不会知道这些铭牌在这里。

00:19:14.830 --> 00:19:38.150
<v Eric Klinenberg>Right here. All these thousands of people who lived alone and died alone aren't alone anymore. Of course, being mourned is a kind of privilege, one that only comes to people who are actually close to other people. Whether they lived alone by choice or by accident, Mary Ann and those 1,918 dead won't get that luxury.
<v Eric Klinenberg>就在这儿。所有这些数千名孤独生活、孤独离世的人们不再孤独了。当然，被哀悼是一种特权，这种特权只属于那些实际上与他人亲近的人。无论他们是出于选择还是偶然独居，Mary Ann和那1918名死者都得不到这种奢侈。

00:19:38.150 --> 00:19:47.270
<v Eric Klinenberg>[MUSIC - "TENUOUS GEARS" BY MICALAVERA]
<v Eric Klinenberg>[音乐 - "TENUOUS GEARS" BY MICALAVERA]

00:19:47.270 --> 00:20:14.280
<v Ira Glass>Eric Klinenberg is a professor of sociology at NYU and is working on a book called Alone in America . Thea Chaloner did the reporting at Evergreen Cemetery in Los Angeles. In the end, Mary Ann's family said that they're going to step in and take care of everything after her death. They'd be responsible for burying her or cremating her. And they'd clean out her house where, a month after her death, there was still a working phone line, and an answering machine.
<v Ira Glass>Eric Klinenberg是纽约大学社会学教授，正在撰写一本名为《美国孤独》的书。Thea Chaloner在洛杉矶常青公墓做了报道。最终，Mary Ann的家人表示他们会介入并处理她死后的一切。他们将负责埋葬或火化她。他们会清理她的房子，在那里，她去世一个月后，仍有一条工作的电话线和一台答录机。

00:20:14.280 --> 00:20:20.970
<v Ira Glass>[PHONE RINGING]
<v Ira Glass>[电话响声]

00:20:20.970 --> 00:20:28.450
<v Mary Ann>This is a message phone. No name, no number, no message, no answer.
<v Mary Ann>这是一条留言电话。没有名字，没有号码，没有消息，无人应答。

00:20:28.450 --> 00:20:30.440
<v Mary Ann>[MESSAGE BEEP]
<v Mary Ann>[信息提示音]

00:20:30.440 --> 00:20:47.710
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, so you're 15 and living by yourself in an apartment, and you need money. So you and a 12-year-old named [? Moochie ?] go in on an investment scheme. That's in a minute, from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，如果你15岁，独自住在公寓里，需要钱。所以你和一个叫[? Moochie ?]的12岁孩子一起搞了一个投资计划。稍后继续，来自芝加哥公共广播和公共广播国际，我们的节目即将继续。

00:20:47.710 --> 00:21:02.050
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. Today on our program, Home Alone. And we have, in the second half of our show, two adventure stories of things that happened to people who were home alone. We've arrived at Act Two of our show. Act Two, Boy Interrupted.
<v Ira Glass>这是《This American Life》。我是Ira Glass。今天我们的节目主题是《独自在家》。在节目的下半部分，我们有两个关于人们独自在家时发生的冒险故事。我们现在进入节目的第二幕。第二幕，《被中断的男孩》。

00:21:02.050 --> 00:21:11.130
<v Ira Glass>Before we get to what Clevins Browne actually did once he was living completely on his own as a teenager, a couple words about how he ended up that way. Sarah Koenig talked to Clevins.
<v Ira Glass>在我们谈到Clevins Browne作为一个青少年完全独自生活时究竟做了什么之前，先说说他是如何走到这一步的。Sarah Koenig采访了Clevins。

00:21:11.130 --> 00:21:26.270
<v Sarah Koenig>When he was little, Clevins Browne moved all around New York with his mother, to different apartments or homeless shelters. He says a few months was the longest they ever stayed anywhere. His mom would get in a fight with someone, or have a bad experience in a neighborhood, and they were off.
<v Sarah Koenig>小时候，Clevins Browne随母亲在纽约四处搬家，去不同的公寓或无家可归者收容所。他说几个月是他待在任何地方的最长时间。他妈妈会和某人吵架，或者在社区里有过糟糕的经历，然后他们就离开了。

00:21:26.270 --> 00:21:41.990
<v Sarah Koenig>But all that changed when Clevins was 12. That's when they finally got a subsidized apartment in Brooklyn, in a public housing complex known as the Pink Houses. Clevins says it was the first time he felt like maybe he could be a normal kid. He could invite friends over. He could decorate his own room.
<v Sarah Koenig>但当Clevins 12岁时，一切都变了。那时他们终于在布鲁克林的一栋补贴公寓里安了家，那是一个被称为“粉红之家”的公共住宅区。Clevins说这是他第一次觉得也许他能成为一个正常孩子。他可以邀请朋友过来。他可以装饰自己的房间。

00:21:41.990 --> 00:22:08.720
<v Clevins Browne>I was putting up posters of Stone Cold Steve Austin and The Rock. And I also had posters of Kid Rock and Limp Bizkit decorating our refrigerator, stuff like that, stuff that we never really got to do, because since we didn't really own the house we can't really do that. But I just felt a sense of pride to say, hey, this is my wall. I can put whatever I want on my wall, because I own this will. Like, wow, I've never owned a wall before.
<v Clevins Browne>我贴上了Stone Cold Steve Austin和The Rock的海报。我还贴了Kid Rock和Limp Bizkit的海报装饰冰箱，诸如此类，我们从未真正做过的事情，因为既然我们不真正拥有这房子，我们就不能那样做。但我感到一种自豪感，说，嘿，这是我的墙。我可以在我的墙上贴任何我想贴的东西，因为我拥有这面墙。就像，哇，我从来没拥有过一面墙。

00:22:08.720 --> 00:22:21.270
<v Sarah Koenig>Clevins says he was happy in the Pink Houses. He liked school, and he had a couple of good friends in the project. And then one day in the spring, when he had just turned 15 and was about to graduate from eighth grade, his mom got sick.
<v Sarah Koenig>Clevins说他在“粉红之家”很开心。他喜欢学校，在项目里有几个好朋友。然后在一个春日，他刚满15岁，即将从八年级毕业，他妈妈病了。

00:22:21.270 --> 00:22:46.500
<v Clevins Browne>We were just watching television. We were watching WB. That's basically what my mom and I do every night. We watch the WB, or we watch UPN. And we were watching this one episode of The Fresh Prince . My mother and I have seen this episode a million times. And a joke came on. I forgot exactly what Will Smith said, but it was just one of those jokes that Will Smith said that we would always laugh at, and I just found myself, that I was the only person laughing.
<v Clevins Browne>我们只是在看电视。我们在看WB。那基本上就是我和我妈妈每天晚上做的事。我们看WB，或者看UPN。我们正在看这一集《新鲜王子妙事多》。我和我妈妈看过这一集一百万次了。然后出现了一个笑话。我忘了Will Smith确切说了什么，但那只是Will Smith说的我们总是会笑的那种笑话，但我发现，只有我一个人在笑。

00:22:46.500 --> 00:23:16.490
<v Clevins Browne>And then my mother was just staring at the television blankly. And I said, Mom, you didn't think that was funny this time? And she just remained quiet. And then she asked me to make her some tea, which really, in my house, with her is really skeptical, because my mother never makes me do anything for her. So when she asked me to make her tea, I felt something was wrong. So while I was making her the tea, I just heard her-- I just heard, like, a boom sound. Like, she just hit the floor.
<v Clevins Browne>然后我妈妈只是呆滞地盯着电视。我说，妈，这次你不觉得好笑吗？她仍然保持沉默。然后她让我给她泡杯茶，这在我的家里，对她来说真的很反常，因为我妈妈从不让我为她做任何事。所以当她让我为她泡茶时，我觉得出了什么问题。所以当我在给她泡茶时，我听到她——我听到，像是，砰的一声。就像，她重重地摔在了地上。

00:23:16.490 --> 00:23:43.260
<v Clevins Browne>Next thing you know, I walk into the room and then my mother is just holding her stomach. And she's closing her eyes and clenching her teeth. She's screaming and everything. Now my first initial reaction is to call 911 and run to the house phone, but then I realized that the house phone is disconnected. So I run downstairs and I find a pay phone, and I called 911. The operator responded by saying, 911, what's your emergency?
<v Clevins Browne>接下来你知道的，我走进房间，然后我妈妈只是捂着肚子。她闭着眼睛，咬着牙。她在尖叫，等等。现在我最初的反应是打911，跑到家里的电话旁，但我意识到家里的电话断线了。所以我跑下楼找到一个公用电话，我打了911。接线员回答说，911，您有什么紧急情况？

