00:26:01.840 --> 00:26:45.980
<v Clevins Browne>I was crying. And then moments later I'm hearing sirens, and I'm looking out the window. So I told her that the ambulance are here for you. Don't tell them that I'm here. And at this point I just ran into the closet. I was really panicking right now, because I said to myself, Here they come. I can hear their footsteps, and I'm hearing walkie talkies and static. So I just closed the door in the closet. And then, at that point, I was pretty much home alone. They were gone. I'm looking out the window, watching them drive along and everything. And I'm just like, I actually pulled this off. I don't believe that I did that.
<v Clevins Browne>我在哭。然后片刻之后我听到警笛声，我看着窗外。所以我告诉她，救护车是来找你的。别告诉他们我在这里。就在这时我跑进了壁橱。我当时真的很恐慌，因为我对自己说，他们来了。我能听到他们的脚步声，还能听到对讲机和静电声。所以我只是关上了壁橱门。然后，在那一刻，我基本上算是独自在家了。他们离开了。我看着窗外，看着他们开车离去等等。我简直不敢相信我真的成功了。我不相信我做到了。

00:26:45.980 --> 00:27:20.900
<v Sarah Koenig>Clevins' mother had a serious stomach illness. Clevins says it was a cyst. But after that was taken care of, she ended up in the psychiatric ward. Clevins doesn't like to talk about it much, but his mom has a pretty long history of mental illness, depression, which accounts for all the moving. She'd been hospitalized for it before. This time, she'd be away for about five months, but Clevins had no way of knowing that or anything about her condition. She couldn't call. Their phone wasn't working. And so he really had no idea what had just happened, where they'd taken her. He barely slept that first night. The next morning, everything just seemed weird.
