<v Clevins Browne>I went to Marie and I asked her if she would like to buy some cigarettes. And she's like, What you doing selling cigarettes for? I'm like, It's not like I'm selling drugs. These are cigarettes. If the store can sell it, why can't I? So she buys it. And she's like, how much do you want for them? I'm like, $2. So she bought one box. Next thing you know, I have a whole line of people just knocking on my door, asking for cigarettes. And in that one night, I made about $100.
<v Clevins Browne>我去找Marie，问她是否想买一些香烟。她说，你卖什么香烟呢？我说，我又不是在卖毒品。这是香烟。如果商店能卖它，为什么我不能？所以她买了。她说，你想要多少钱？我说，2美元。所以她买了一盒。接下来你知道的，我有一整排的人在敲我的门，问我要香烟。在那一个晚上，我赚了大约100美元。

00:32:18.050 --> 00:32:47.990
<v Sarah Koenig>Like any good businessman, Clevins invested some of that money into a new venture, and made a profit of $150, with the help of his friend, [? Moochie. ?] [? Moochie ?] was about 12, but he was shrewd. And he convinced Clevins, the way to make more money was to throw a party at his house and charge people $7 to come. [? Moochie ?] arranged the whole thing, the boom box, the food, which his mom cooked. The clean up and security detail, all [? Moochie's ?] little friends. And it worked, and Clevins was getting by, mostly staying out of trouble.
<v Sarah Koenig>像任何优秀的商人一样，Clevins把其中的一些钱投入了一个新的风险项目，并在他的朋友[? Moochie ?]的帮助下赚了150美元的利润。[? Moochie ?]大约12岁，但他很精明。他向Clevins建议，赚更多钱的方法是在他家里开个派对，向每个人收取7美元入场费。[? Moochie ?]安排了这一切，音箱，食物，他妈妈做的。清理工作和安保细节，全是[? Moochie ?]的小朋友们。成功了，Clevins靠这个应付了过去，大多待在远离麻烦的地方。

00:32:47.990 --> 00:32:51.550
<v Sarah Koenig>And then at about the two-month mark, he found a permanent solution to the food problem.
<v Sarah Koenig>然后在大约两个月的时候，他找到了解决食物问题的永久方法。

00:32:51.550 --> 00:32:56.360
<v Clevins Browne>There is this thing called an EBT card. EBT cards are food stamps.
<v Clevins Browne>有个东西叫EBT卡。EBT卡就是食品券。

00:32:56.360 --> 00:32:58.120
<v Sarah Koenig>How did you find the card?
<v Sarah Koenig>你是怎么找到这张卡的？

00:32:58.120 --> 00:32:59.290
<v Clevins Browne>Going through my mother's stuff.
<v Clevins Browne>翻我妈妈的东西。

00:32:59.290 --> 00:33:00.620
<v Sarah Koenig>Where was it?
<v Sarah Koenig>它在哪儿？

00:33:00.620 --> 00:33:03.190
<v Clevins Browne>It was inside her purse that she left.
<v Clevins Browne>在她留下的钱包里。

00:33:03.190 --> 00:33:04.640
<v Sarah Koenig>Oh, so she didn't even have her purse with her.
<v Sarah Koenig>噢，所以她连钱包都没带在身边。

00:33:04.640 --> 00:33:05.700
<v Clevins Browne>No.
<v Clevins Browne>没有。

00:33:05.700 --> 00:33:07.030
<v Sarah Koenig>Oh my God.
<v Sarah Koenig>天哪。

00:33:07.030 --> 00:33:32.470
<v Clevins Browne>So basically my mother had this card, and basically that's where all our food was coming from, like she would take this card, go to supermarkets. So I was always under the impression that the code-- because there's a code, a four-digit number code-- I was always under the impression that it was my mother's birthday, 9763. So I took the card to a supermarket, and I swiped the card and I have all this bunch of food and everything.
<v Clevins Browne>所以基本上我妈妈有这张卡，而基本上那就是我们所有食物的来源，就像她会拿这张卡，去超市。所以我一直以为密码——因为有个密码，四位数字密码——我一直以为是我妈妈的生日，9763。所以我带这张卡去了超市，我刷了卡，拿了一堆食物等等。

00:33:32.470 --> 00:33:33.720
<v Sarah Koenig>What had you picked out?
<v Sarah Koenig>你选了什么？

00:33:33.720 --> 00:33:52.480
<v Clevins Browne>I had picked out tons of pop tarts, cereal, peanut butter and jelly, bread, and all that. And I just throw it on the counter, and I swipe the card in. And I type in 9763. But then the card said, "invalid entry. Read error."
<v Clevins Browne>我选了成吨的果酱馅饼、麦片、花生酱和果冻、面包，诸如此类。我只是把它扔在柜台上，刷了卡。我输入了9763。但卡片说，“无效输入。读取错误。”

00:33:52.480 --> 00:34:10.620
<v Clevins Browne>And before I figured out the access code, I would always go down to the supermarket and just run a test. I would take one item, and see if I could possibly pay for it by entering this access code. So I'm trying to figure it out, and I'm just scavenging through all of her stuff.
<v Clevins Browne>在我弄明白访问密码之前，我总是去超市做测试。我会拿一样商品，看看如果我输入这个访问密码是否可能付钱。所以我试着弄明白它，我就在她的所有东西里搜寻。

00:34:10.620 --> 00:34:55.950
<v Clevins Browne>And my mother has this leather jacket that is a unisex jacket, and I always wanted to wear this jacket. I personally think the jacket looks better on me than it does on her. And I would take this jacket. I would pretend that I'm Shaft. And I had these two cap guns, these two black toy cap guns that I had. So what I would do is I would go in front of the mirror and put the guns inside my coat pocket and pull them out and just start shooting them. And I'm just going through the rest of the pockets. So then I found an envelope that I was about to throw out. And I read it, and it was a letter from the food stamps company, reminding my mother that her access code is this, this, this.
<v Clevins Browne>我妈妈有一件皮夹克，是一件中性夹克，我一直想穿这件夹克。我个人认为夹克穿在我身上比穿在她身上好看。我会拿这件夹克。我会假装我是Shaft。我有两把爆竹枪，我有的那两把黑色玩具爆竹枪。所以我怎么做呢，我会在镜子前，把枪放在外套口袋里，拔出来，然后开始开枪。我只是在翻其他口袋。然后我找到一个我正要扔掉的信封。我读了它，那是来自食品券公司的一封信，提醒我妈妈她的访问密码是这个，这个，这个。

00:34:55.950 --> 00:35:14.490
<v Clevins Browne>And to my surprise, I went down to the supermarket and purchased some food, and it was actually the correct number. Now, what the food stamps card does is that they give you cash and they give you food stamps. I had about $200 in cash and $200 in food stamps. So I was really excited and grateful that this happened.
<v Clevins Browne>令我惊讶的是，我去了超市买了一些食物，那真的是正确的数字。现在，食品券卡所做的是，他们给你现金，他们给你食品券。我有大约200美元现金和200美元食品券。所以我真的很兴奋和感激发生了这件事。

00:35:14.490 --> 00:35:36.340
<v Sarah Koenig>All this time, his mom's close friend, a woman Clevins calls Aunt Elizabeth, one of the few adults he'd told about his situation, had been calling around to different hospitals trying to track down his mother. Eventually, she did. Clevins found an old prescription of his mom's, and Aunt Elizabeth called the number and there she was, at Brookdale Hospital in Brooklyn. Clevins rode his bike to go see her. It was pretty upsetting.
<v Sarah Koenig>这期间，他妈妈的好朋友，一个Clevins叫Elizabeth姨妈的女人，他告诉过的少数几个大人之一，一直在给不同的医院打电话试图追踪他妈妈。最终，她做到了。Clevins找到了他妈妈的一个旧处方，Elizabeth姨妈拨通了那个号码，她就在那儿，在布鲁克林的Brookdale医院。Clevins骑自行车去看她。情况相当令人难过。

00:35:36.340 --> 00:36:02.230
<v Clevins Browne>There were some other people in there who had some really serious mental issues in there. And it really just made me feel uncomfortable. The people that she's in the ward with, those people are talking to themselves. And I'm always asking myself, why is my mother even with these people? She's really nothing like them. Why is she considered to be one of them? My mother's pretty sane, but these guys are the total opposite.
<v Clevins Browne>那里还有其他人有一些非常严重的精神问题。这真的让我感到不舒服。她所在的病房里的那些人，那些人正在跟自己说话。我一直在问自己，为什么我妈妈甚至和这些人在一起？她真的和他们一点也不像。为什么她被认为是他们中的一员？我妈妈相当清醒，但这些人完全相反。

00:36:02.230 --> 00:36:04.130
<v Sarah Koenig>Did you ask her that?
<v Sarah Koenig>你问她了吗？

00:36:04.130 --> 00:36:06.680
<v Clevins Browne>No.
<v Clevins Browne>没有。

00:36:06.680 --> 00:36:09.360
<v Sarah Koenig>Do you feel like you know why, now?
<v Sarah Koenig>你觉得你现在知道为什么了吗？

00:36:09.360 --> 00:36:19.540
<v Clevins Browne>Not really. Still trying to figure that out.
<v Clevins Browne>说真的没。还在试着弄清楚这一点。

00:36:19.540 --> 00:36:32.160
<v Sarah Koenig>By this time, the summer was almost over. And even though this whole thing had been hard on Clevins, still, in another way, he had lived the 15-year-old's dream summer. Unlimited freedom, nothing to do but hang out with his friends.
<v Sarah Koenig>到这个时候，暑假快结束了。即使整件事对Clevins来说很难，但从另一方面来说，他过了一个15岁孩子梦想中的暑假。无限的自由，除了和朋友闲逛什么都不用做。

00:36:32.160 --> 00:36:50.550
<v Sarah Koenig>But now he had to start high school all on his own. He was supposed to go to South Shore High School, which he knew nothing about, not even how to get there. He'd never even really been on the subway without his mom. That first day, he asked the train conductor directions to his school, as if the guy was his personal tour guide. He never made it.
<v Sarah Koenig>但现在他必须独自开始上高中了。他应该去South Shore高中，他对那儿一无所知，甚至不知道怎么去那儿。他甚至从未在没他妈妈的情况下真正坐过地铁。第一天，他问火车售票员去他学校的方向，好像那家伙是他私人导游一样。他没成功。

00:36:50.550 --> 00:37:06.100
<v Sarah Koenig>But on day two, once Clevins did get there, he was immediately miserable. The place was huge. He says it was like a jungle. And it was so easy to cut classes, which he did, sometimes to go visit his mom. His grades slipped. Most of all, he hated how people at his school saw him.
<v Sarah Koenig>但在第二天，一旦Clevins确实到了那儿，他立刻就感到很痛苦。那个地方很大。他说这就像个丛林。而且逃课太容易了，他也逃了，有时去探望他妈妈。他的成绩下降了。最重要的是，他讨厌学校里的人如何看待他。

00:37:06.100 --> 00:37:14.200
<v Clevins Browne>I didn't want to walk in to school being this panicky kid with problems. That's not who I wanted to be. I just wanted to be a regular student.
<v Clevins Browne>我不想作为一个有问题的恐慌孩子走进学校。那不是我想成为的人。我只想做一个普通学生。

00:37:14.200 --> 00:37:24.880
<v Sarah Koenig>Did you feel like people would look at you and know, oh, that kid. Nobody's taking care of-- did you look different from other kids? Did you feel like you looked different? Were your clothes dirty or something?
<v Sarah Koenig>你有没有觉得人们会看着你并知道，噢，那个孩子。没人照顾……你看起来和别的孩子不一样吗？你有没有觉得你看起来不一样？你的衣服是不是脏了之类的？

00:37:24.880 --> 00:37:33.670
<v Clevins Browne>My clothes were dirty, actually. And I was really antisocial. I didn't really mix myself with other students and all that.
<v Clevins Browne>我的衣服实际上是脏的。而且我真的很反社会。我没怎么和其他学生混在一起等等。

00:37:33.670 --> 00:37:36.610
<v Sarah Koenig>Because why?
<v Sarah Koenig>为什么？

00:37:36.610 --> 00:38:10.980
<v Clevins Browne>I just didn't feel like-- it just wasn't in me. I felt like I didn't know what direction I was going in. It's like you're being teased. Like, you have something in front of your eyes, but you can't get a hold of it. I'm always able to see my mother and talk to her, but I don't know when she's going to come out. I don't know how long she's going to be there. And that really just drove me insane. I don't know what to do right now. And I never talked to anyone. Never talked to my teachers. There could be times where a teacher-- I'm sitting in a class and a teacher would call my name-- and I would just ignore them, like they're not even there. Like, don't talk to me.
<v Clevins Browne>我只是觉得——我只是没那个心情。我觉得我不知道我正朝什么方向走。就像被嘲弄一样。就像，你眼前有东西，但你抓不住它。我总能见到我妈妈并和她说话，但我不知道她什么时候会出来。我不知道她还要在那儿待多久。那真的让我抓狂。我现在不知道该怎么办。而且我从来没跟任何人谈过。从来没跟我的老师谈过。有时老师——我正坐在课上，老师会叫我的名字——而我只是无视他们，就像他们根本不在那儿一样。就像，别跟我说话。

00:38:10.980 --> 00:38:14.500
<v Sarah Koenig>Were are you feeling self-conscious, that people would find out?
<v Sarah Koenig>你是不是感到自卑，怕人们会发现？

00:38:14.500 --> 00:38:18.890
<v Clevins Browne>Yeah. I was barely washing my clothes.
<v Clevins Browne>是的。我几乎不洗衣服。

00:38:18.890 --> 00:38:21.000
<v Sarah Koenig>Could you tell? Could people tell?
<v Sarah Koenig>看得出来吗？人们看得出来吗？

00:38:21.000 --> 00:38:22.280
<v Clevins Browne>Yeah.
<v Clevins Browne>看得出来。

00:38:22.280 --> 00:38:24.540
<v Sarah Koenig>Did anyone ever say anything about it?
<v Sarah Koenig>有人说起过吗？

00:38:24.540 --> 00:38:39.480
<v Clevins Browne>My guidance counselor. She just said, what's going on? I could obviously see something's wrong with you. That's really a sign of something going on wrong at home, when people don't wash their clothes.
<v Clevins Browne>我的指导顾问。她只是说，怎么了？我显然能看出你出了问题。当人们不洗衣服时，那真的是家里出了问题的迹象。

00:38:39.480 --> 00:38:43.030
<v Sarah Koenig>What was your response to that?
<v Sarah Koenig>你的反应是什么？

00:38:43.030 --> 00:38:58.580
<v Clevins Browne>I was just like, are you just attacking me? Is that what it is? Until now, I didn't see it-- I saw it as a way, oh, she's just attacking me. She's just trying to judge and disrespect me. So I'm just going to leave her alone. Forget about it. Forget you, too.
<v Clevins Browne>我只是说，你是在攻击我吗？这就是吗？直到现在，我没看出来——我把它看作一种方式，噢，她只是在攻击我。她只是想评判和不尊重我。所以我决定离她远点。忘了它吧。你也滚蛋吧。

00:38:58.580 --> 00:39:00.730
<v Sarah Koenig>So did you not go see her again?
<v Sarah Koenig>所以你没再去见她了吗？

00:39:00.730 --> 00:39:01.610
<v Clevins Browne>No, I didn't.
<v Clevins Browne>没，我没去。

00:39:01.610 --> 00:39:03.510
<v Sarah Koenig>And it was because she said that?
<v Sarah Koenig>是因为她说了那些话吗？

00:39:03.510 --> 00:39:04.670
<v Clevins Browne>Mm-hm.
<v Clevins Browne>嗯。

00:39:04.670 --> 00:39:09.750
<v Sarah Koenig>And then, one day, the hospital called Aunt Elizabeth and told her Clevins' mom was ready to come home.
<v Sarah Koenig>然后，有一天，医院打电话给Elizabeth姨妈，说Clevins的妈妈准备回家了。

00:39:09.750 --> 00:39:14.400
<v Clevins Browne>Prior to that day, the house was extremely messy, and my mother comes home.
<v Clevins Browne>在那天之前，家里极其凌乱，而我妈妈回家了。

00:39:14.400 --> 00:39:15.410
<v Sarah Koenig>Were you worried?
<v Sarah Koenig>你担心吗？

00:39:15.410 --> 00:39:33.190
<v Clevins Browne>Yeah. I thought she was really going to freak out with the way the house looked. Actually, right before I opened the door, I'm like, Mom, you have to brace yourself for what you're about to see. There are just so many unholy things going on in this house right now that you do not want to look at.
<v Clevins Browne>担心。我以为她真的会因为房子的样子而崩溃。实际上，就在我开门之前，我想，妈，你得为你即将看到的做好心理准备。这屋里现在有太多肮脏的事在发生，你不会想看的。

00:39:33.190 --> 00:39:38.500
<v Sarah Koenig>Describe how messy we're talking.
<v Sarah Koenig>描述一下有多乱？

00:39:38.500 --> 00:40:15.360
<v Clevins Browne>If you opened the door, imagine you're walking outside on a snowy day, and you have to struggle your ankles to get through the snow. Just substitute the snow with clothes and potato chip bags. And I thought she was just going to be really upset. I was like, Mom, I'm going to take care of this. This is my mess. I'll take care of it. No need to get mad or anything. She didn't get upset. But I looked in her eyes and I felt like this is probably her way, a small way, of making up for the time she spent gone. So that's why she really, really wanted to clean. I've left you alone for this long. This is the least I could possibly do for you.
<v Clevins Browne>如果你打开门，想象你在下雪天走到户外，你得费力地挪动脚踝才能穿过积雪。只要把雪换成衣服和薯片袋就行了。我以为她会真的很生气。我说，妈，我会处理的。这是我的烂摊子。我会处理的。没必要生气之类的。她没生气。但我看着她的眼睛，我觉得这可能是她弥补她离开的时间的一种方式，一种小小的弥补。所以我真的很、很想清理。我把你留在这儿这么久。这是我能为你做的最起码的事情。

00:40:15.360 --> 00:40:34.650
<v Clevins Browne>So I came back just to check up on her. The house is completely spotless. There was no evidence of my five months of garbage piling up in there. She just got rid of it all. The clothes were neatly packed up, the garbage is gone, the floor is mopped and swept. I'm like, what?
<v Clevins Browne>所以我回来只是看看她。房子完全一尘不染。没有我那五个月垃圾堆积在那儿的任何痕迹。她只是把它们全处理掉了。衣服被整齐地收好，垃圾消失了，地板也被拖过和扫过了。我说，什么？

00:40:34.650 --> 00:40:41.520
<v Sarah Koenig>Did she ever say anything, directly, about the time you'd been alone? Did you guys ever have a conversation about it?
<v Sarah Koenig>她有没有直接提到你独自待在那儿的那段时间？你们谈过这件事吗？

00:40:41.520 --> 00:40:50.670
<v Clevins Browne>Yeah, she kept on apologizing. I said, there's no need for you to apologize. You didn't intentionally say you want to leave me for five months. So it's not your fault.
<v Clevins Browne>有，她一直在道歉。我说，你没必要道歉。你不是有意说你想抛下我五个月的。所以这不是你的错。

00:40:50.670 --> 00:41:00.940
<v Sarah Koenig>Was there any part of you that sort of-- Like, obviously, you love your mom and you wanted her to come home, but did you worry that certain things would become harder if she came home, too?
<v Sarah Koenig>有没有哪一部分你某种程度上——显然，你爱你的妈妈，你想让她回家，但你是否担心如果她回家了，某些事情也会变得更难？

00:41:00.940 --> 00:41:16.630
<v Clevins Browne>I felt that, when she comes home, I felt like I'm just going to be in the same predicament again one day. I don't know when. But I just knew and felt that it's going to happen again one day. And I just felt like I just want to be more prepared the next time it happens.
<v Clevins Browne>我觉得，当她回家时，我觉得有一天我会再次陷入同样的困境。我不知道什么时候。但我只是知道并感觉到有一天会再次发生。所以我只是觉得下次发生时我只想准备得更充分一些。

00:41:16.630 --> 00:41:32.150
<v Sarah Koenig>A few years later, it did happen again. His mom disappeared. Clevins was evicted. But this time, Clevins was 18, and he did handle it a lot better. Since then, Clevins has been living in a friend's apartment. He's working now, and he's planning on going to college next year.
<v Sarah Koenig>几年后，确实又发生了。他妈妈失踪了。Clevins被驱逐了。但这次，Clevins 18岁了，他处理得好多了。从那时起，Clevins一直住在朋友的公寓里。他现在在工作，并计划明年上大学。

00:41:32.150 --> 00:41:35.590
<v Sarah Koenig>[MUSIC - "SPACE KEY" BY DANIEL LANOIS]
<v Sarah Koenig>[音乐 - "SPACE KEY" BY DANIEL LANOIS]

00:41:35.590 --> 00:41:45.030
<v Ira Glass>Sarah Koenig is one of the producers of our show.
<v Ira Glass>Sarah Koenig是我们节目的制片人之一。

00:41:45.030 --> 00:42:02.330
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, The Man Who Came to Dinner. Well, we end our show today with the story of a mother who was home alone with her two small children, when one of the scariest things that can happen actually happens. This was in the early 1980s. Jennifer Schaller was one of the two little children at the time, and she tells the story.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，《共进晚餐的人》。好吧，今天我们以一个母亲独自带着两个小孩在家，这时发生了最可怕的事情之一来结束我们的节目。这是在80年代初。Jennifer Schaller当时是两个小孩之一，她讲述了这个故事。

00:42:02.330 --> 00:42:20.450
<v Jennifer Schaller>There's a story that my mother doesn't like to tell, because it brings back too many bad memories for her. But I love to hear her tell it. It captures her perfectly, how fierce she is. It also shows what was really going on when I was a kid, and I didn't even know it.
<v Jennifer Schaller>有一个我妈妈不喜欢讲的故事，因为它带回了太多糟糕的回忆。但我喜欢听她讲。它完美地捕捉到了她的性格，她有多么坚强。它也展示了当我还是个孩子时真正发生了什么，而我甚至不知道。

00:42:20.450 --> 00:42:38.100
<v Jennifer Schaller>We were living in Hightstown, New Jersey, in a one-bedroom apartment in a big courtyard building. It was fall. My brother Alan and I were home from school playing outside. I was five or six, and Alan was a year younger. My mom, Ezra, picks up the story from here.
<v Jennifer Schaller>我们住在新泽西州的Hightstown，在一栋大型庭院建筑的一居室公寓里。那是秋天。我和我哥哥Alan放学在家在外面玩。我五六岁，Alan小一岁。我妈妈Ezra从这儿开始讲。

00:42:38.100 --> 00:42:56.810
<v Ezra>I was in the house by myself. And then, he knocks on the door, and I open it up. And he identifies himself as one of Raymond's friends, or associates, as they call each other.
<v Ezra>我独自在家里。然后，他敲门，我打开了。他自称是Raymond的朋友之一，或者他们所谓的同事。

00:42:56.810 --> 00:43:38.950
<v Jennifer Schaller>Raymond is my father. He wasn't around much. My parents told me that it was because of his job as a salesman. I imagined he was a traveling salesman who pushed appliances. I found out later that he was a pusher, but his job had nothing to do with vacuums or dishwashers. When my mother and father first met, he was a legitimate jewelry salesman. Then he traveled to Peru for his father's funeral, and while he was there his half-brother introduced him to the world of cocaine trafficking. The drugs were a constant source of tension between my father and mother, who begged him to stop selling. He promised he would, but never did.
<v Jennifer Schaller>Raymond是我父亲。他很少在家。我父母告诉我这是因为他做推销员的工作。我以为他是推销家电的旅行推销员。我后来发现他是个毒贩，但他的工作跟吸尘器或洗碗机毫无关系。当我母亲和父亲初次见面时，他是合法的珠宝推销员。后来他去秘鲁参加父亲的葬礼，在那里他的同父异母兄弟把他带进了可卡因走私的世界。毒品是我父亲和我母亲之间持续不断的紧张来源，我母亲恳求他停止贩毒。他答应会停，但从来没停过。

00:43:38.950 --> 00:43:53.340
<v Jennifer Schaller>At the time this story takes place, Raymond had been gone for more than a week, and my mom didn't know where he was. The man at the door told my mother he had business to discuss with Raymond. He wasn't going to leave until they had settled the matter.
<v Jennifer Schaller>在这个故事发生时，Raymond已经离开一周多了，我妈妈不知道他在哪儿。门口的那个男人告诉我母亲他有生意要和Raymond讨论。在解决这件事之前，他是不会走的。

00:43:53.340 --> 00:44:15.680
<v Ezra>And at that moment, both you and Alan run up behind him to see who it was that was at the door. And I really didn't know what to say to you guys. So he said that he was actually going to be staying with us for a while. And you guys were satisfied with that. And, you know, Oh, cool. And you just take off and you start playing with your cousins again.
<v Ezra>就在那一刻，你和Alan跑在他身后看是谁在门口。我真的不知道该对你们说什么。所以他说他实际上会和我们待上一段时间。你们满足于此了。而且，你知道，噢，酷。你们就走开，又开始和表亲们玩了。

00:44:15.680 --> 00:44:25.160
<v Ezra>And so, at that point, he comes inside the apartment, and he shows me the gun.
<v Ezra>所以在那一点上，他进了公寓，他向我展示了枪。

00:44:25.160 --> 00:44:50.130
<v Jennifer Schaller>The gunman said he had to make a phone call, so my mother took him to the phone in the kitchen. She listened to his conversation. It became clear that he was on the phone with his boss in Florida. She overheard him report that Raymond was not around. He said my mom didn't know where my father was or how to get a hold of him. Then he turned to my mom and said his boss wanted to speak with her.
<v Jennifer Schaller>持枪歹徒说他必须打个电话，所以我母亲带他到厨房的电话旁。她听了他的谈话。很清楚他在和佛罗里达的老板通话。她无意中听到他报告Raymond不在。他说我妈不知道我父亲在哪儿或怎么联系他。然后他转向我妈说他的老板想和她谈谈。

00:44:50.130 --> 00:45:32.260
<v Ezra>The man from Florida never identified himself, never actually gave his name. He just proceeded to tell me that he was very disappointed with Raymond, and that Raymond had disappeared. It'd been four or five days already. Absolutely no communication with him. He had a kilo of coke on him, and it belonged to the man from Florida. So he was concerned about his merchandise and his money. So this is why that man wound up at our doorstep, to help smoke Raymond out.
<v Ezra>来自佛罗里达的男人从没表明身份，甚至从没报上名字。他只是继续告诉我是的，他对我父亲Raymond非常失望，而且Raymond失踪了。已经四五天了。和他完全没有沟通。他身上有一公斤可卡因，属于佛罗里达的男人。所以他很担心他的商品和他的钱。所以这就是那人落到我们门口的原因，为了帮忙把Raymond引出来。

00:45:32.260 --> 00:45:52.110
<v Ezra>I hang up the phone with the guy from Florida, after he had told me that he was going to kill my children and me if Raymond didn't show up. I hang up the phone with the guy and I tell him, well, I guess we might as well get comfortable, because it looks like you're going to be here for a while, because Raymond isn't going to show up anytime soon.
<v Ezra>我在和佛罗里达那家伙打完电话后挂断了，在他告诉我会杀了我孩子和我如果Raymond没出现的话。我挂了电话告诉他说，好吧，我想我们不妨让自己舒服点，因为看起来你要在这里待上一段时间，因为Raymond不会很快出现。

00:45:52.110 --> 00:46:19.250
<v Jennifer Schaller>When my mom tells the story today, there's a lot of bravado. But at the time, she was terrified, mainly for us. She wanted to keep me and my brother physically safe, but she didn't want this episode to scar us emotionally either. And so she made a decision. She would continue with the ruse as long as she needed to. She carried on normally, as if the gunmen really was an old friend, staying with us for a short time.
<v Jennifer Schaller>当我母亲今天讲这个故事时，有很多虚张声势。但在当时，她非常害怕，主要是为了我们。她想保持我和我哥哥的身体安全，但她也不想让这段插曲在情感上伤害我们。所以她做了一个决定。只要需要，她会继续这场骗局。她照常生活，就像歹徒真的是个老朋友，在我们这儿做客几天。

00:46:19.250 --> 00:47:09.530
<v Ezra>That evening you guys came in, I made dinner for us. And you and your brother just joked around, because you thought he was a friend of your dad's. So you guys were just being yourselves and joking around and stuff. And I put you guys to bed. And after I put you guys to bed, I went into the bathroom, and I grabbed a towel and I bunched it up and put it on my face. And I started screaming into it, as quietly as I could. And then I started crying. And then, I thought to myself, OK, that's it. This guy can't know that you're upset or that you're fearful. Because the moment he sees fear, he's going to think he's got control. So he's not going to get that control from me.
<v Ezra>那天晚上你们进来，我给我们做了晚饭。你和你哥哥只是开玩笑，因为你们以为他是你爸爸的朋友。所以你们只是做回自己，开开玩笑之类的。我把你们送上床睡觉。在我把你们送上床后，我去了浴室，我抓了一条毛巾，把它揉成一团放在脸上。我开始对着它尖叫，尽可能安静。然后我开始哭。然后，我想对自己说，好吧，就是这样。这家伙不能知道你在心烦或害怕。因为他一看到恐惧，他就会认为他掌控了局面。所以他不会从我这儿得到那种控制权。

00:47:09.530 --> 00:47:21.270
<v Jennifer Schaller>But how does a single mother with two small kids gain control over a hired gunman? Moments after her tears in the bathroom, my mother saw an opportunity brought about by the television.
<v Jennifer Schaller>但一个有两个小孩的单亲妈妈怎么能控制一个受雇的歹徒呢？在她浴室泪水流下的片刻后，我母亲看到了电视带来的机会。

00:47:21.270 --> 00:47:33.400
<v Ezra>I wash my face, and I go back out into the living room, and I sat down. And, at that time, it was probably about 8 or 9 o'clock, a movie started on TV, and it was The Godfather.
<v Ezra>我洗了脸，我回到客厅，我坐了下来。那时，可能大约8点或9点，电视上开始了一部电影，是《教父》。

00:47:33.400 --> 00:47:37.020
<v Jennifer Schaller>I know. The Godfather. Can you believe it?
<v Jennifer Schaller>我知道。教父。你能相信吗？

00:47:37.020 --> 00:48:14.160
<v Ezra>I started conversing with the guy, and I tell him. I says, yeah, you know. I have an uncle that's in the mafia. As I was telling him this story, his eyes got bigger. And he gets up quietly, and he goes over to the telephone, and he calls the guy in Florida. He's explaining to him, you know, I'm not sure that we should be here in this place with this woman, because she has connections. And he was very nervous. I could see that. And then he comes and he sits down. And I just kept talking, like nothing. And I saw that I had the upper hand at that moment, so I was going to just go with it.
<v Ezra>我开始和那家伙交谈，我告诉他。我说，是啊，你知道。我有一个叔叔在黑手党里。当我对他讲这个故事时，他的眼睛变大了。他悄悄起身，走到电话旁，打电话给佛罗里达那家伙。他在向他解释，你知道，我不确定我们是否应该和这个女人待在这个地方，因为她有关系。他非常紧张。我看得出来。然后他走过来坐下。我只是继续说话，就像什么事都没发生一样。我看到在那一刻我占了上风，所以我打算就这样继续下去。

00:48:14.160 --> 00:48:40.620
<v Jennifer Schaller>My mother's uncle actually wasn't in the mafia, but his family all believed he was because that's what he told them. He was actually hiding a girlfriend, and later on another family, from his wife. He was a polygamist and not a goodfella. This lie struck a nerve with the hired gunman, who had settled in and slept nights on the sofa. My mother waited on him, bringing him things to eat and drink.
<v Jennifer Schaller>我母亲的叔叔其实没在黑手党里，但他的全家都相信他在，因为他就是这么告诉他们的。他实际上是对妻子隐瞒了一个女朋友，后来还有一个家庭。他是一夫多妻制，不是个好人。这个谎言触动了受雇歹徒的神经，他在沙发上安顿下来，晚上就睡在那儿。我母亲侍奉他，带给他东西吃和喝。

00:48:40.620 --> 00:48:46.990
<v Ezra>The next day, you guys go to school, and I spend the whole entire day with this guy in our apartment.
<v Ezra>第二天，你们去学校了，我和这家伙在我们公寓里待了一整天。

00:48:46.990 --> 00:48:49.820
<v Jennifer Schaller>What did you guys do? Just watch TV?
<v Jennifer Schaller>你们做什么了？只是看电视吗？

00:48:49.820 --> 00:49:13.330
<v Ezra>Pretty much just watched TV, yeah. And, every hour or two hours, the man from Florida would call and try and intimidate me and harass me, asking me where Raymond was and threatening to kill us. And I said, well, I don't know what to tell you, man. But you do what you got to do. If you feel that you got to kill us, then I guess I don't have any control over that.
<v Ezra>基本上就是看电视，是的。然后，每隔一两小时，佛罗里达那男人就会打来电话试图恐吓和骚扰我，问我Raymond在哪儿，威胁要杀了我们。我说，好吧，我不知道该跟你说什么，老兄。但你做你必须做的。如果你觉得你必须杀了我们，那么我想我对此没有任何控制权。

00:49:13.330 --> 00:49:45.810
<v Jennifer Schaller>This is the point my mother says where her fear turned to rage. She was enraged at my dad for getting us into this mess, at her captor for his intrusion, and at the man on the other end of the phone for his constant threats. So she amped things up a notch. While watching an episode of Miami Vice , she casually mentioned a cousin who was a police detective. This, my mother said, made her captor seem even more nervous. Then she began working on an excuse to see my aunt and uncle who lived in the apartment next door.
<v Jennifer Schaller>这就是我母亲说恐惧转为愤怒的那一点。她对我爸把我们带进这堆烂摊子感到愤怒，对俘虏她的歹徒的入侵感到愤怒，也对电话那头不断威胁的人感到愤怒。所以她把事情升级了。在看《迈阿密风云》一集时，她随口提到了一个当警察侦探的表亲。我母亲说，这让她俘虏的歹徒看起来更加紧张了。然后她开始寻找借口去见住在隔壁公寓的姑妈和姑父。

00:49:45.810 --> 00:50:21.360
<v Ezra>I had run out of milk. And the guy was insisting that I couldn't go anywhere, and that he had to watch me at all times. And I said well, cool, that's fine, but we need milk for the kids. And I asked him, I said, OK, you won't allow me to go to the store. Can I go right next door to my aunt's house and just ask her for a container of milk? And he just thought about it, and thought about it, and he waves his hand at me and says go ahead, go ahead, but you better be fast. Come back, and you better not be longer than five minutes.
<v Ezra>我的牛奶用完了。那家伙坚持说我哪儿也不能去，而且他必须时刻看着我。我说好吧，酷，那很好，但我们需要牛奶给孩子喝。我问他，我说，好吧，你不准我去商店。我可以去隔壁我姑妈家，只要问她要一盒牛奶吗？他只是想了想，又想了想，他对我挥挥手说去吧，去吧，但你最好快点。回来，你最好别超过五分钟。

00:50:21.360 --> 00:50:42.380
<v Ezra>So I go next door, and I tell my uncle what's going on. And my uncle right away grabs his rifle, one of his rifles, and he says, well, I'm going over there and we're going to take care of this. And I said well, no, I don't want you getting involved in this. I don't want anything to happen to you. So I said, can I use your phone? Let me call Tito real quick.
<v Ezra>所以我去隔壁，我告诉姑父发生了什么。我姑父立刻抓起他的步枪，他的一把步枪，他说，好吧，我要过去，我们要处理这件事。我说好吧，不，我不想让你参与进来。我不希望你出事。所以我说，我能用一下你的电话吗？让我快点打给Tito。

00:50:42.380 --> 00:51:16.000
<v Jennifer Schaller>Tito was an old family friend. He was working as a delivery man at the time. My mother didn't call the police for two reasons. She didn't want my father to go to jail, and she also feared that her children would be caught up in a violent mess. She called Tito to ask him to drop by so that our captor would know that we had people, that we were not alone. Things ended up working out better than she thought. The gunman mistakenly assumed that Tito was the police detective my mother mentioned earlier.
<v Jennifer Schaller>Tito是一个老家人的朋友。他当时是一名送货员。我母亲没报警有两个原因。她不想让我父亲坐牢，她也害怕她的孩子会卷入一场暴力冲突中。她打电话给Tito请求他顺道来看看，这样我们的俘虏就会知道我们有自己人，我们不是独自一人的。事情最终比她预想的要好。歹徒错误地认为Tito就是我母亲提到的那个警察侦探。

00:51:16.000 --> 00:52:17.600
<v Ezra>I guess it was because of the clothing that he was wearing. He had on a dark navy blue t-shirt and dark navy blue pants. And he didn't have any type of insignia on his clothing. And so, Tito comes in and he sits down. I introduce him to the guy from Florida. And Tito's, you know, Hi, I just thought I'd come by and see how my comrade's doing, and find out how the kids are doing. I haven't talked to them in a while. So the guy kind of slowly weens his way back towards the telephone in the kitchen, and he's on the phone again. And I tell Tito. I says, Tito, shh. Listen to what he's saying. And he was in a panic. He says, I'm leaving. I am not staying. You do whatever the hell it is that you need to do, but I'm getting the hell out of here because now her cousin, who's the detective from New York City, is here. What the hell am I supposed to do now?
<v Ezra>我想是因为他穿的衣服。他穿了一件深海军蓝T恤和深海军蓝裤子。他的衣服上没有任何徽章。所以Tito进来了，他坐下。我把他介绍给了佛罗里达那家伙。Tito说，你知道，嗨，我只是想顺道过来看看我的伙伴怎么样了，看看孩子们怎么样了。我有一阵子没和他们说话了。所以那家伙有点慢慢挪回到厨房的电话旁，他又在打电话了。我告诉Tito。我说，Tito，嘘。听他在说什么。他陷入了恐慌。他说，我走了。我不会待在这儿。随你妈的便你想做什么就做什么，但我正在赶快离开这儿，因为现在她的表亲，那个纽约市的侦探，在这儿。我现在到底该怎么办？

00:52:17.600 --> 00:52:40.820
<v Jennifer Schaller>The man on the other end of the phone somehow talked him into staying, and he did for three more days, with me and my brother coming and going to school and my mother waiting on him hand and foot. But by this point, the only person in the house who seemed afraid was the man with the gun, which brings us to what might be my mother's greatest triumph.
<v Jennifer Schaller>电话那头的人不知怎么说服他留了下来，他确实又多待了三天，我和我哥哥来来回回上学，我母亲对他伺候得无微不至。但到了这个时候，屋里唯一看起来害怕的人就是那个拿枪的人，这让我们谈到了可能是我母亲最大的胜利。

00:52:40.820 --> 00:53:21.660
<v Jennifer Schaller>My brother and I never suspected any of this was going on. When I think back on this time, I remember going to kindergarten every day. I liked to hug my teacher. My favorite food was pizza, and I was the best speller in my class. I have no recollection of being held hostage by a gunman. Neither does my brother. As a child, I was never angry at my dad for running off with someone else's cocaine. And I don't think this memory is repressed. I think my mother wanted more than anything for my brother and me to believe that our household was like any other, and she succeeded. I only learned what happened that week years later, when I was grown.
<v Jennifer Schaller>我和哥哥从未怀疑过这一切正在发生。当我回想起那个时候，我记得每天去幼儿园。我喜欢拥抱我的老师。我最喜欢的食物是披萨，我是班上拼写最好的。我不记得被持枪歹徒劫持过。我哥哥也不记得。作为一个孩子，我从来没有因为我爸爸带着别人的可卡因跑掉而生他的气。而且我不认为这段记忆是被压抑了。我认为我母亲最想要的是让我和哥哥相信我们的家庭和其他家庭一样，而且她成功了。我直到多年后成年才了解到那天发生了什么。

00:53:21.660 --> 00:53:49.550
<v Ezra>I didn't show or demonstrate any anxiety. Whenever you guys were around, I was my normal self with you. I would play with you. I would feed you guys. I would read a book to you before you went to bed. And I went about business normally, our daily lives as I normally would. That was pretty much it. That's maybe why you don't remember it ever happening.
<v Ezra>我没表现或展示出任何焦虑。每当你们在场时，我跟你们在一起表现得很正常。我会陪你们玩。我会喂你们。我会在你们睡觉前读一本书。我照常生活，像我通常那样处理我们的日常生活。基本就是这样。那可能就是为什么你们不记得它发生过的原因。

00:53:49.550 --> 00:54:09.460
<v Jennifer Schaller>On our fourth day as hostages, my father showed up with the money and an explanation which seemed to placate the man who took us hostage, but not my mother. This ordeal stamped out any hope she'd had of reconciling with my dad. The man with the gun was about to leave when my mother went into the backyard.
<v Jennifer Schaller>在我们作为人质的第四天，我父亲带着钱和一个解释出现了，这似乎安抚了劫持我们的人，但并没有安抚我母亲。这种磨难消灭了她与我父亲和解的任何希望。那个拿枪的人正要离开，这时我母亲走到后院。

00:54:09.460 --> 00:54:57.550
<v Ezra>I had stepped outside into the backyard. I mean, after so many days of being stuck inside the house, I needed fresh air. So I stepped outside, and so the man steps outside. And he says to me, you know, senora, I am so sorry. I hope that you are not going to hold this against me and tell your cousins or your uncle to come after me, because I really didn't want to be a part of this. I was forced into this situation, and ma'am, I really, really, really am sorry about this whole thing. And he grabs my hand, and I thought he was going to shake my hand. He grabs my hand, and he kissed it. That was when I thought, damn, I'm good.
<v Ezra>我走到后院。我的意思是，被困在屋里这么多天后，我需要新鲜空气。所以我走到外面，然后那男人也走到外面。他对我说，你知道，夫人，我真的很抱歉。我希望你不会因为这个怀恨在心，或者让你的表亲或姑父来追杀我，因为我真的不想参与其中。我是被迫卷入这种情况的，夫人，我真的、真的、真的很抱歉整件事。他抓住我的手，我以为他要握我的手。他抓住我的手，亲吻了它。就在那时我想，该死，我真棒。

00:54:57.550 --> 00:55:20.270
<v Ezra>That was the moment, truthfully. That was it. That was when I thought, damn. And to this day, I think about it, and my palms get all sweaty. I was really in the zone. I had my control back. I had taken it back. And it felt great. It really did. It felt really good.
<v Ezra>在那一刻，说实话。就是这样。就在那时我想，该死。直到今天，每当我想到它，手心就会出汗。我真的进入了状态。我重新掌握了控制权。我夺回了它。感觉棒极了。真的，感觉真好。

00:55:20.270 --> 00:56:02.090
<v Jennifer Schaller>My father went on selling drugs for years. When I was 11, he went to prison. But my mother says this experience changed her. Nothing rattles her even now, she says. Not long ago, a client-- my mom's an insurance agent-- became irate with her, and, from across the desk in her office, threatened her with bodily harm. My mom, all of five foot two, says she stood up, stepped out from behind her desk, and said calmly to the man, Well, have at it. The man walked out of office in a huff and never bothered her again.
<v Jennifer Schaller>我父亲继续卖毒品卖了好多年。在我11岁时，他进了监狱。但我母亲说这次经历改变了她。她说，即使现在也没什么能震住她。不久前，一位客户——我妈是保险经纪——对她大发雷霆，在办公室桌子对面威胁要对她造成身体伤害。我妈，五英尺二英寸高，说她站起来，从办公桌后面走出来，冷静地对那男人说，好吧，来吧。那男人气冲冲地走出了办公室，再也没找过她的麻烦。

00:56:02.090 --> 00:56:15.850
<v Ira Glass>Jennifer Schaller lives in New Mexico.
